; Test: What are "cux", "result", "counted"?
; ===========================================
;
; They're just DUMMY SYMBOLS with no special meaning!
; COUNT uses them as template shapes to count unique combinations.

; Setup
(foo 1) (foo 2)
(bar x) (bar y)

; These THREE execs do the EXACT SAME THING with different dummy symbols:

; Test 1: Using "cux" (from MORK source tests)
(exec (0 test-cux)
  (, (foo $a) (bar $b))
  (O (count (with-cux $k) $k (cux $a $b))))

; Test 2: Using "result" (from our earlier examples)
(exec (0 test-result)
  (, (foo $a) (bar $b))
  (O (count (with-result $k) $k (result $a $b))))

; Test 3: Using "totally-arbitrary-symbol"
(exec (0 test-arbitrary)
  (, (foo $a) (bar $b))
  (O (count (with-arbitrary $k) $k (totally-arbitrary-symbol $a $b))))

; What happens in ALL THREE cases:
; 1. Input matches 4 times: (1,x), (1,y), (2,x), (2,y)
; 2. COUNT instantiates template 4 times:
;    - (cux 1 x), (cux 1 y), (cux 2 x), (cux 2 y)  [Test 1]
;    - (result 1 x), (result 1 y), ...             [Test 2]
;    - (totally-arbitrary-symbol 1 x), ...          [Test 3]
; 3. All 4 instantiations are unique → count = 4
; 4. Reports: (with-cux 4), (with-result 4), (with-arbitrary 4)

; Expected output:
; (with-cux 4)
; (with-result 4)
; (with-arbitrary 4)
;
; The template symbols are NEVER added to the space!
; They only exist inside COUNT's internal PathMap.
;
; You can use ANY symbol you want: foo, bar, baz, counted, placeholder, etc.
; The symbol choice doesn't matter - only the SHAPE matters for counting uniqueness.
